\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Convergence curves on a single \texttt  {funnel} instance, for illustration only (taken from a diagnostic batch of three instances; see Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:batches}). The horizontal axis is wall-clock time; each curve is the time-wise mean over three seeds, and the shaded band is the seed-to-seed \emph  {range} (the sample is too small to estimate quartiles). The solid lines (B1, B2) are always realized makespans; the dashed line is the surrogate objective of the open-loop search, i.e.\ the quantity it actually optimizes; crosses and pluses mark the makespan of the same plan after execution by conflict-free routing. The gap between the end of the dashed line and the cross is the optimism margin on this instance: the open loop has not failed to converge; it has converged to a surrogate objective that cannot be realized.}}{30}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:convergence}{{3}{30}{Convergence curves on a single \texttt {funnel} instance, for illustration only (taken from a diagnostic batch of three instances; see Table~\ref {tab:batches}). The horizontal axis is wall-clock time; each curve is the time-wise mean over three seeds, and the shaded band is the seed-to-seed \emph {range} (the sample is too small to estimate quartiles). The solid lines (B1, B2) are always realized makespans; the dashed line is the surrogate objective of the open-loop search, i.e.\ the quantity it actually optimizes; crosses and pluses mark the makespan of the same plan after execution by conflict-free routing. The gap between the end of the dashed line and the cross is the optimism margin on this instance: the open loop has not failed to converge; it has converged to a surrogate objective that cannot be realized}{figure.caption.24}{}}
