\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Per-instance results of the four-level ladder and the two effects, closed loop and dispatching, blocked by the three families (each instance has $n=10$ comparisons paired by seed). Significance marks follow the \textbf  {Holm-corrected} $p$ (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test), the family being the $10$ instances of one effect: $^{*}p<0.05$, $^{**}p<0.01$; $^{\dagger }$ means significant before correction but not after, and the sign of that instance is not taken as a conclusion. A negative value means a lower makespan. The two gain columns use the same estimator as Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:main} (the mean of pairwise relative gains over seeds) and cannot be obtained by dividing the two means in the same row. \texttt  {A funnel} is at once $N_A/N_M=1.5$ of Family\nonbreakingspace B and $F=0.6$ of Family\nonbreakingspace C; it is run only once and occupies only one row.}}{32}{table.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bytag}{{8}{32}{Per-instance results of the four-level ladder and the two effects, closed loop and dispatching, blocked by the three families (each instance has $n=\NSeeds $ comparisons paired by seed). Significance marks follow the \textbf {Holm-corrected} $p$ (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test), the family being the $\NCases $ instances of one effect: $^{*}p<0.05$, $^{**}p<0.01$; $^{\dagger }$ means significant before correction but not after, and the sign of that instance is not taken as a conclusion. A negative value means a lower makespan. The two gain columns use the same estimator as Table~\ref {tab:main} (the mean of pairwise relative gains over seeds) and cannot be obtained by dividing the two means in the same row. \texttt {A funnel} is at once $N_A/N_M=1.5$ of Family~B and $F=0.6$ of Family~C; it is run only once and occupies only one row}{table.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The closed loop and dispatching across the three families. Left: layout family, horizontal axis contention intensity; middle: vehicle-to-arm ratio, $N_A/N_M$; right: flexibility. Families\nonbreakingspace B and\nonbreakingspace C include the shared base point ($1.5$ and $0.6$, at the hollow vertical lines), which is also listed as \texttt  {A funnel} in Family\nonbreakingspace A. Filled markers remain significant after Holm correction ($p<0.05$, the family being the $10$ instances of one effect); hollow markers are not significant. Error bars are the standard error of the mean of pairwise relative gains over the $10$ seeds of each instance. The closed loop is significant on 9{} instances and rises roughly with contention; after correction dispatching is significant on \emph  {no instance}, its error bars cross zero on every instance, and its sign alternates across the four levels of the vehicle-to-arm ratio. The point of the figure is whether the two curves deviate significantly from zero, not the shape of the dispatching curve.}}{32}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:prediction3}{{4}{32}{The closed loop and dispatching across the three families. Left: layout family, horizontal axis contention intensity; middle: vehicle-to-arm ratio, $N_A/N_M$; right: flexibility. Families~B and~C include the shared base point ($1.5$ and $0.6$, at the hollow vertical lines), which is also listed as \texttt {A funnel} in Family~A. Filled markers remain significant after Holm correction ($p<0.05$, the family being the $\NCases $ instances of one effect); hollow markers are not significant. Error bars are the standard error of the mean of pairwise relative gains over the $\NSeeds $ seeds of each instance. The closed loop is significant on \CellsSigLoopAdj {} instances and rises roughly with contention; after correction dispatching is significant on \emph {no instance}, its error bars cross zero on every instance, and its sign alternates across the four levels of the vehicle-to-arm ratio. The point of the figure is whether the two curves deviate significantly from zero, not the shape of the dispatching curve}{figure.caption.30}{}}
