\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces A fixed wall-clock budget places the cost per evaluation and the number of evaluations completed on a hyperbola; the grey line is the $90$-second cap shared by all configurations. A point on the line means that cost $\times $ count equals that configuration's wall-clock time. The two closed-loop configurations lie on the line, that is, every run uses the full budget. The open loop lies \emph  {below} it: it stops improving after a little over four hundred generations and is ended by early stopping, converging in about $17$ seconds rather than being cut off by the budget (Section\nonbreakingspace \ref {sec:protocol}). B0 and B0$^+$ share the same open-loop search and therefore share one point; they differ only in how the schedule is executed. Pale small points are the individual measurements on three instances, and the large filled point is their geometric mean: on a log axis, only the geometric mean preserves ``cost $\times $ count $=$ that configuration's wall-clock time''. Orange markers are the off and on settings of pruning and winner-path reuse (the callout marks the ``on'' setting). By Proposition\nonbreakingspace \ref {prop:speedup_equiv} the two produce identical output, so the displacement along the line reflects only the compute saved.}}{37}{figure.caption.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocol}{{6}{37}{A fixed wall-clock budget places the cost per evaluation and the number of evaluations completed on a hyperbola; the grey line is the $90$-second cap shared by all configurations. A point on the line means that cost $\times $ count equals that configuration's wall-clock time. The two closed-loop configurations lie on the line, that is, every run uses the full budget. The open loop lies \emph {below} it: it stops improving after a little over four hundred generations and is ended by early stopping, converging in about $17$ seconds rather than being cut off by the budget (Section~\ref {sec:protocol}). B0 and B0$^+$ share the same open-loop search and therefore share one point; they differ only in how the schedule is executed. Pale small points are the individual measurements on three instances, and the large filled point is their geometric mean: on a log axis, only the geometric mean preserves ``cost $\times $ count $=$ that configuration's wall-clock time''. Orange markers are the off and on settings of pruning and winner-path reuse (the callout marks the ``on'' setting). By Proposition~\ref {prop:speedup_equiv} the two produce identical output, so the displacement along the line reflects only the compute saved}{figure.caption.38}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Switching the two speed-ups on and off.}{37}{section*.41}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The same set of comparisons under the two budget conventions. Each row is named by the mechanism it isolates. The difference between the two markers comes from the convention: reservation-table-aware dispatching is 2.90\%{} ($p=0.0269$) at an equal number of generations and 0.03\%{} ($p=0.8799$), close to zero, at equal wall-clock time. The batch ran before the two speed-ups (Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:batches}), which is also why the speed-ups are switched on and off in a separate control below.}}{38}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocols}{{7}{38}{The same set of comparisons under the two budget conventions. Each row is named by the mechanism it isolates. The difference between the two markers comes from the convention: reservation-table-aware dispatching is \GainProbeSameGen {} ($p\PProbeSameGen $) at an equal number of generations and \GainProbeSameClock {} ($p\PProbeSameClock $), close to zero, at equal wall-clock time. The batch ran before the two speed-ups (Table~\ref {tab:batches}), which is also why the speed-ups are switched on and off in a separate control below}{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Changing only pruning and winner-path reuse. ``Off''/``on'' indicates whether the two are enabled; the bold header marks the setting adopted in this paper. The two settings produce bit-identical output (verified by the equivalence self-check), so the difference comes only from the compute saved. Same instances, same seeds, same wall-clock time; $n=60$ pairs, two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test ($^{***}p<0.001$); the aggregate row lists wins/losses/ties and $p$. Both ratio columns use the \emph  {off} value as denominator, in opposite directions: evaluations as on/off, time per evaluation as off/on.}}{38}{table.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prune}{{9}{38}{Changing only pruning and winner-path reuse. ``Off''/``on'' indicates whether the two are enabled; the bold header marks the setting adopted in this paper. The two settings produce bit-identical output (verified by the equivalence self-check), so the difference comes only from the compute saved. Same instances, same seeds, same wall-clock time; $n=60$ pairs, two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test ($^{***}p<0.001$); the aggregate row lists wins/losses/ties and $p$. Both ratio columns use the \emph {off} value as denominator, in opposite directions: evaluations as on/off, time per evaluation as off/on}{table.caption.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces B0 (top) and B2 (bottom) on the same funnel instance. Robotic arms and vehicles share the time axis; vehicle rows distinguish empty and loaded legs, as well as two kinds of gap. A gap between the two legs of the same transport task: the vehicle waits at a berth because a corridor is occupied, i.e.\ a yielding wait, which is the delay treated in this paper. A gap between different tasks: the vehicle is idle. The two are shown in different colours so that idle time is not misread as yielding.}}{39}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:case_gantt}{{8}{39}{B0 (top) and B2 (bottom) on the same funnel instance. Robotic arms and vehicles share the time axis; vehicle rows distinguish empty and loaded legs, as well as two kinds of gap. A gap between the two legs of the same transport task: the vehicle waits at a berth because a corridor is occupied, i.e.\ a yielding wait, which is the delay treated in this paper. A gap between different tasks: the vehicle is idle. The two are shown in different colours so that idle time is not misread as yielding}{figure.caption.43}{}}
